            <ArticleTitle>An unusual cause of pain in the hip.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1093/jscr/rjw109</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">rjw109</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Colorectal Carcinoma is the second biggest killer after lung and bronchial carcinoma. Due to its insidious onset and late manifestation of symptoms a national screening programme was started in 2006 within the UK. We present a rare case of colorectal adenocarcinoma mimicking osteosarcoma and presenting as hip pain at initial consultation in the orthopaedic clinic. Further adding to the rarity of this case is that of an apparent bony metastasis in isolation. Our case demonstrates the vital role that histological sampling plays in the diagnosis and treatment of carcinomatous disease. It also demonstrates the need to maintain high index of clinical suspicion, no matter how benign the presenting complaint may seem.</AbstractText>
